WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.522 --> 00:00:03.204
- Whitney was on a national
team with people I looked up to,

00:00:03.204 --> 00:00:05.474
and, you know, she'd
always come back with,

00:00:05.474 --> 00:00:08.008
like, autographs and stuff, and
I was like, I want to do that.

00:00:08.008 --> 00:00:11.023
I'm getting 52 more practices
than everybody else is,

00:00:11.023 --> 00:00:12.741
and that's going to make that little

00:00:12.741 --> 00:00:14.389
our big difference in the end.

00:00:14.389 --> 00:00:15.973
You know, if you want to do something

00:00:15.973 --> 00:00:19.410
and you want it that bad,
you'll make sacrifices.

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:22.125
You know the people before
me set the bar so high,

00:00:22.125 --> 00:00:24.991
and I was a little kid that wasn't afraid

00:00:24.991 --> 00:00:27.131
to dream as big as I could.

00:00:27.131 --> 00:00:29.036
- He's an American competitive swimmer

00:00:29.036 --> 00:00:32.594
and the most decorated
Olympian athlete of all time.

00:00:32.594 --> 00:00:35.951
He also holds the all time
record for Olympic gold medals.

00:00:35.951 --> 00:00:38.323
His international titles and
record breaking performances

00:00:38.323 --> 00:00:39.653
has earned him the title

00:00:39.653 --> 00:00:42.702
of World Swimmer of the Year seven times.

00:00:42.702 --> 00:00:44.381
He's Michael Phelps, and here's my take

00:00:44.381 --> 00:00:46.337
on his top ten rules for success.

00:00:46.337 --> 00:00:48.185
Rule number three is my personal favorite,

00:00:48.185 --> 00:00:50.834
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:50.834 --> 00:00:52.917
(swoosh)

00:00:54.023 --> 00:00:56.503
- I had two older sisters
that I kind of looked up to,

00:00:56.503 --> 00:01:00.024
and for them, having the chance to travel

00:01:00.024 --> 00:01:01.742
nationally and internationally, you know,

00:01:01.742 --> 00:01:03.728
Whitney was on an international team

00:01:03.728 --> 00:01:06.124
with people I looked up to, and, you know,

00:01:06.124 --> 00:01:08.332
she'd always come back with,
like, autographs and stuff,

00:01:08.332 --> 00:01:10.050
and I was like, I want to do that.

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:12.096
I want to be on those teams.
I want to travel the world,

00:01:12.096 --> 00:01:14.370
and at that point, I was just like,

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:16.249
I want to be an Olympic Gold medalist.

00:01:16.249 --> 00:01:17.628
I want to break a world record,

00:01:17.628 --> 00:01:19.680
and I want to be a professional athlete,

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:24.078
and those goals that I
thought about every single day

00:01:24.078 --> 00:01:27.731
kept me coming, week after
week and year after year

00:01:27.731 --> 00:01:29.517
until I was able to accomplish them.

00:01:29.517 --> 00:01:31.347
- [Announcer] What struck
us most about Michael

00:01:31.347 --> 00:01:33.429
when we met him all those years ago

00:01:33.429 --> 00:01:36.607
was his dedication to the
long hours in the pool.

00:01:36.607 --> 00:01:38.811
- It's one reason why we
trained seven days a week,

00:01:38.811 --> 00:01:41.420
why we train on Christmas,
why we train on Thanksgiving,

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:43.571
365 days a year.

00:01:43.571 --> 00:01:45.505
I don't know of many clubs
that train seven days a week.

00:01:45.505 --> 00:01:47.814
So, that gives you one
extra set training session

00:01:47.814 --> 00:01:50.817
every single week where
you can pick something up.

00:01:50.817 --> 00:01:53.475
So, I mean, I'm getting 52 more practices

00:01:53.475 --> 00:01:56.931
than everybody else is, and
that's going to make that little

00:01:56.931 --> 00:01:58.828
our big difference in the end.

00:01:58.828 --> 00:02:01.918
- What do you feel separates good athletes

00:02:01.918 --> 00:02:03.835
from the best athletes?

00:02:05.706 --> 00:02:10.304
- I think it comes back to what
I was saying earlier about,

00:02:10.304 --> 00:02:13.971
I mean, I think it's,
a lot of it is mental.

00:02:14.908 --> 00:02:16.761
You know, if you want to do
something, and you want it

00:02:16.761 --> 00:02:19.511
that bad, you'll make sacrifices,

00:02:20.502 --> 00:02:23.666
and, you know, I, growing
up for me as a kid,

00:02:23.666 --> 00:02:26.489
I made a lot of sacrifices
that I didn't see 'em

00:02:26.489 --> 00:02:29.194
that they were a big deal,
and I don't see 'em, like,

00:02:29.194 --> 00:02:32.406
I don't think of them being,
as being a big deal right now.

00:02:32.406 --> 00:02:34.747
You know, there were Saturdays
or Fridays where, you know,

00:02:34.747 --> 00:02:37.712
I'd give up going to a dance
or hanging out with my friends

00:02:37.712 --> 00:02:40.316
'cause I had workout the
next day, but those things

00:02:40.316 --> 00:02:44.657
that I gave up, you know,
were things that I wanted to,

00:02:44.657 --> 00:02:47.996
to help me get to my
goal, and there were times

00:02:47.996 --> 00:02:52.080
where I been put in
uncomfortable situations,

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:53.864
but I've used my mental power

00:02:53.864 --> 00:02:55.818
and my mental strength to push through it.

00:02:55.818 --> 00:02:57.837
I think that's how you,
you know, you can look

00:02:57.837 --> 00:02:59.560
at any of the athletes in the world.

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:03.560
I mean, Tiger, the guy's
a busted knee and still

00:03:04.471 --> 00:03:06.867
comes and destroys the rest of the field.

00:03:06.867 --> 00:03:08.186
You know, that's the thing.

00:03:08.186 --> 00:03:11.910
It's like, people don't make excuses.

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:13.823
There's some of the best
athlete, or the greatest athletes

00:03:13.823 --> 00:03:17.613
in the world, do things
when they're uncomfortable.

00:03:17.613 --> 00:03:19.900
You know, everybody can compete
when they're feeling good,

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:24.357
happy, excited, rested,
but it's the best people

00:03:24.357 --> 00:03:28.469
who can overcome when they're tired, sore,

00:03:28.469 --> 00:03:32.214
not in the great mind, you
know, not in the best mindset,

00:03:32.214 --> 00:03:36.085
and I think mental power that
the greatest athletes have

00:03:36.085 --> 00:03:38.418
can push all the pain aside.

00:03:39.435 --> 00:03:41.914
You know, I mean, when I was
doing that Navy Seals thing,

00:03:41.914 --> 00:03:44.497
was it easy for me to do it? No.

00:03:44.497 --> 00:03:45.748
Was it easy for me to climb

00:03:45.748 --> 00:03:48.178
over a 45 foot wall and look down?

00:03:48.178 --> 00:03:51.181
Not at all; I'm afraid
of heights, but you know,

00:03:51.181 --> 00:03:53.265
I said to myself, I'm going
to do this and no one's

00:03:53.265 --> 00:03:56.769
going to stop me, and I think
that's the biggest thing

00:03:56.769 --> 00:03:59.081
is how strong you can be mentally

00:03:59.081 --> 00:04:01.549
'cause I think that really
shows who the person is.

00:04:01.549 --> 00:04:03.567
So, I think that's one
thing that separates

00:04:03.567 --> 00:04:05.422
the good from the greatest.

00:04:05.422 --> 00:04:09.501
I think that I just, I
just keep the ball rolling.

00:04:09.501 --> 00:04:11.934
I like to, I guess, have some more

00:04:11.934 --> 00:04:13.470
best times in other events.

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:16.994
Maybe a close score that
gets noticed. I don't know.

00:04:16.994 --> 00:04:20.492
I think that, it's my long term goal

00:04:20.492 --> 00:04:22.539
is to win the gold medal in Athens

00:04:22.539 --> 00:04:24.428
and go in more than one event.

00:04:24.428 --> 00:04:25.684
So, I mean, I have a lot of goals,

00:04:25.684 --> 00:04:28.271
but I think that I just
want to take day-by-day.

00:04:28.271 --> 00:04:30.440
- [Voiceover] Michael
Phelps was a force of nature

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:32.978
who was preparing to take on
the world and win his first

00:04:32.978 --> 00:04:35.821
Olympic medal at that year's Athens games.

00:04:35.821 --> 00:04:39.315
He stood six foot four with a
wing span of six foot seven.

00:04:39.315 --> 00:04:41.883
The then 18 year old had
already established himself

00:04:41.883 --> 00:04:44.722
as the world's fastest
ever all-around swimmer,

00:04:44.722 --> 00:04:47.593
and at this stage in her career,
he held three world records

00:04:47.593 --> 00:04:50.906
and three world titles,
but his big aim was to win

00:04:50.906 --> 00:04:53.689
Olympic gold in Athens, and not just one.

00:04:53.689 --> 00:04:56.534
He was looking to equal or
even exceed Mark Spitz's

00:04:56.534 --> 00:05:00.399
record hold of seven gold
medals from the 1972 Olympics.

00:05:00.399 --> 00:05:02.876
Many, including Australian's Ian Thorpe,

00:05:02.876 --> 00:05:06.706
thought Phelps's mission, impossible.

00:05:06.706 --> 00:05:08.911
- I think if he said that then
it's, I means it's sort of

00:05:08.911 --> 00:05:11.102
he thinks that he can't do it.

00:05:11.102 --> 00:05:14.522
I don't think it's impossible for anybody.

00:05:14.522 --> 00:05:17.365
I mean, I think if you really
just have a wide open mind,

00:05:17.365 --> 00:05:18.794
and use your imagination,

00:05:18.794 --> 00:05:20.783
I think a lot of things are possible.

00:05:20.783 --> 00:05:24.337
I think of myself as the greatest person

00:05:24.337 --> 00:05:26.259
that I can be, and that's it.

00:05:26.259 --> 00:05:28.508
I'm not going to go out of
my, you know, out on a limb

00:05:28.508 --> 00:05:30.641
and say I'm the greatest this or that.

00:05:30.641 --> 00:05:34.263
I just, I was a kid
with a dream, that's it,

00:05:34.263 --> 00:05:38.802
and, you know, the people
before me set the bars so high,

00:05:38.802 --> 00:05:41.637
and I was a little kid that wasn't afraid

00:05:41.637 --> 00:05:44.701
to dream as big as I could,
and that's really it.

00:05:44.701 --> 00:05:46.815
I, you know, like you said,
we were like talking before,

00:05:46.815 --> 00:05:49.164
I was dedicated, I was hardworking,

00:05:49.164 --> 00:05:52.447
and that put me in the
shoes where I am now.

00:05:52.447 --> 00:05:55.436
You know, ever since
I've moved to Arizona,

00:05:55.436 --> 00:05:59.711
it's been so wild to me,
to see so many people

00:05:59.711 --> 00:06:02.695
that have come up and
just been supportive,

00:06:02.695 --> 00:06:04.499
and I think that's
something really special

00:06:04.499 --> 00:06:07.281
because it goes hand, it goes
back to what I wanted to do

00:06:07.281 --> 00:06:11.594
with the sport, and being able
just to know that this person

00:06:11.594 --> 00:06:13.760
or that person is
watching at the random bar

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:18.033
down the street or a random
restaurant down the street.

00:06:18.033 --> 00:06:20.289
I've changed things in the sport,

00:06:20.289 --> 00:06:22.277
and I've done what I wanted to do,

00:06:22.277 --> 00:06:25.205
and there are people back
home, when we're overseas,

00:06:25.205 --> 00:06:27.446
that are screaming their brains out,

00:06:27.446 --> 00:06:30.172
and there's nothing cooler,
like, when I come home

00:06:30.172 --> 00:06:33.172
and hear just the stories from them,

00:06:34.725 --> 00:06:36.644
and those little times, you know,

00:06:36.644 --> 00:06:40.426
could change someone's life,
could pick somebody up.

00:06:40.426 --> 00:06:42.006
It's just an honor for me.

00:06:42.006 --> 00:06:44.155
- This time his retirement is for real,

00:06:44.155 --> 00:06:47.338
even though he claimed the
same thing after London.

00:06:47.338 --> 00:06:49.740
I remember saying to you, "Are you done?"

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:51.198
You said, "I'm done, I'm done."

00:06:51.198 --> 00:06:53.818
I said, "The great ones
never retire," Jordan.

00:06:53.818 --> 00:06:55.193
- The million dollar question, I know.

00:06:55.193 --> 00:06:57.537
- Sugar Ray, you said, "Nope, nope, nope,"

00:06:57.537 --> 00:06:58.905
but you were boned.

00:06:58.905 --> 00:07:00.678
You weren't loving the
swimming at that time.

00:07:00.678 --> 00:07:03.635
- I hated it; I wanted nothing
to do with the sport then,

00:07:03.635 --> 00:07:06.384
and that's why I came
back 'cause I didn't end

00:07:06.384 --> 00:07:09.440
my career like that, but
I had to find the passion,

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:13.420
myself, again, and I found
that again, and I had fun,

00:07:13.420 --> 00:07:15.604
and this was, this is
how I wanted to finish.

00:07:15.604 --> 00:07:17.582
How I, what I did here is the best.

00:07:17.582 --> 00:07:20.051
- What happened in, since London and Rio

00:07:20.051 --> 00:07:23.993
in those couple of years that
turned the lights back on?

00:07:23.993 --> 00:07:27.741
- I guess I just started
being me and found who I was

00:07:27.741 --> 00:07:30.206
and was happy with who I am,
and that's what everybody's

00:07:30.206 --> 00:07:32.746
going to see, and that's
what everyone is seeing

00:07:32.746 --> 00:07:36.160
over the last two years,
me being me and being a kid

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:38.841
and loving what I'm doing, and, you know,

00:07:38.841 --> 00:07:40.377
being able to start a family.

00:07:40.377 --> 00:07:42.583
There were a lot of mixed
feelings after that race.

00:07:42.583 --> 00:07:47.113
I wasn't happy with the
time, but afterwards, I like,

00:07:47.113 --> 00:07:49.990
I saw the time and was like,
man, that's really, like,

00:07:49.990 --> 00:07:52.584
I'm not happy with that,
and then I realized

00:07:52.584 --> 00:07:55.917
that I'd made my fifth Olympic team, and

00:07:58.596 --> 00:08:02.677
you know, that was
something that after London,

00:08:02.677 --> 00:08:05.295
I didn't think was possible.

00:08:05.295 --> 00:08:07.862
Taking some time off, going
through some ups and downs,

00:08:07.862 --> 00:08:10.623
and being able to get through
the obstacles that I had to be

00:08:10.623 --> 00:08:15.028
able to have the chance to
swim on my fifth Olympic team.

00:08:15.028 --> 00:08:18.483
It's just special to not
only be on the team again

00:08:18.483 --> 00:08:21.876
and represent my country,
but also have the chance

00:08:21.876 --> 00:08:25.959
to have my son come and
watch my last swims ever.

00:08:27.832 --> 00:08:29.188
He might not remember 'em,

00:08:29.188 --> 00:08:31.277
but I'll have the memories for life.

00:08:31.277 --> 00:08:33.836
I don't, I don't really live
the normal life as a teenager.

00:08:33.836 --> 00:08:37.033
I mean, I get a lot of,
I give, I guess I give up

00:08:37.033 --> 00:08:39.912
a lot of things, but, I mean,
I'm getting tons and tons

00:08:39.912 --> 00:08:43.000
of other things that normal
15 year olds aren't getting.

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:45.503
So, I think that, I think this is the life

00:08:45.503 --> 00:08:48.655
that anyone could dream of,
and I'm definitely loving it.

00:08:48.655 --> 00:08:49.946
- Thank you guy so much for watching.

00:08:49.946 --> 00:08:53.678
I made this video because
sam229a asked me to.

